6	################################################################################
7	## Namespace for Lattice IP packaging:
8	#  * These are mainly some xml tree manipulator procedures.
9	#  * The base descriptors for Lattice IP xml's and interface xml's.
10	#  * The Lattice IP specific procedures to configure these descriptors.
11	#  * There is another xml generator procedure and the folder generator
12	#    procedures for IPs and IP interfaces.
13	#  * There are mainly four descriptor structures for IP related xml's.
14	#  * These four structures are wrapped up in a $::ipl::ip structure with file
15	#    dependencies added to them for holding all the necessary IP data.
16	#  * There are two more descriptors for interface xml's wich are wrapped up too
17	#    in a single $::ipl::if structure and hold all the necessary interface data.
18	#
19	# There are two main parts of procedures and structures in this namespace:
20	#
21	#  1. IP related procedures and descriptors for users:
22	#     * $::ipl::ip
23	#       - This describes an IP itself. It is used to set a new IP instance like:
24	#                                                            'set ip $::ipl::ip'
25	#       - After the instance is correctly configured, it is used to generate an
26	#         actual IP on a specified path.
27	#       - This instance is updated by every IP related procedure like:
28	#         set ip [<some_ip_procedure> -ip $ip]' except the 'ipl::generate_ip'
29	#         and 'ipl::parse_module'.
30	#     * ipl::general
31	#       - sets the IP structure with the specified general IP parameters.
32	#     * ipl::parse_module
33	#       - This module is used to parse the data of the IP top module, it's input
34	#         parameter is the file path of the top module and it returns a structure
35	#         with the top module's data which is parameter for other procedures.
36	#     * ipl::add_ports_from_module
37	#       - it is used to set the IP structure with the port's data from the
38	#         module data which is set by 'ipl::parse_module'
39	#     * ipl::add_memory_map
40	#       - sets the IP structure with a new memory map, the name of this memory
41	#         map must be used for slave memory mapped interface configuration
42	#     * ipl::add_address_space
43	#       - sets the IP structure with an address space, the name of this address
44	#         space must be used for master memory mapped interface configuration
45	#     * ipl::add_axi_interfaces
46	#       - automatically adds AXI interfaces based on parsed module data from top
47	#         module
48	#     * ipl::add_interface
49	#       - sets the IP structure with an interface instance.
50	#     * ipl::add_interface_by_prefix
51	#       - sets the IP structure with an interface by filtering ports by prefix
52	#         from module data parsed with ipl::parse_module when a naming standard
53	#         like <verilog_prefix>_<standard_port_name> is used.
54	#     * ipl::add_ip_files
55	#       - sets the IP structure with IP file dependencies
56	#     * ipl::add_ip_files_auto
57	#       - sets the IP structure with the specified file dependencies by
58	#         searching them in the specyfied '-spath' folder '-sdepth' deep.
59	#     * ipl::set_parameter
60	#       - sets the IP structure with a configuration parameter which will appear
61	#         in the IP GUI also.
62	#     * ipl::ignore_ports
63	#       - ignores/hides a list of ports by a Python expression which usually
64	#         depends on the value of a Verilog parameter.
65	#     * ipl::ignore_ports_by_prefix
66	#       - ignores/hides ports which are matching with a specified prefix from
67	#         the ports' names in the parsed ports from the top module, by a
68	#         Python expression which usually depends on the value of a Verilog
69	#         parameter.
70	#     * ipl::generate_ip
71	#       - generates the IP on specified path, if no path parameter, then in
72	#         default IP download directory of Lattice Propel Builder.
73	#
74	#  2. Custom IP interface related descriptors and procedures for users:
75	#     * $::ipl::if
76	#       - This describes an IP interface structure, it is used to set a new
77	#         interface instance like: 'set if $::ipl::if'
78	#     * ipl::create_interface
79	#       - creates a custom interface
80	#     * ipl::generate_interface
81	#       - generates a custom interface from the structure set by
82	#         ipl::create_interface
83	################################################################################
